  "query_preview": "..An upper low currently centered over northern \nChihuahua, Mexico will continue to move east today as a ridge of \nhigh pressure begins to build into the region from the west. The \nridge will amplify across the western United States over the \nweekend, with the ridge axis nearly overhead Sunday into Monday. The \nridge axis will then slide to our east on Tuesday through Thursday \nof next week.  \n  \nA strong warming trend will occur through Monday as the ridge builds \ninto the region. High temperatures will warm to 10-15 degrees above \nnormal Sunday and Monday. Isolated areas from Tucson westward will \nbe in the Major category for HeatRisk on Monday, which is expected \nto be the hottest day. Highs Monday will be 101-103 degrees in the \nTucson metro and around 105 degrees in the far western deserts near \nAjo and Organ Pipe. Breezy conditions will occur through the early \nevening hours today in the upper Gila River valley around Safford \nand again Saturday afternoon/evening, with wind speeds of 15-20 mph \nand gusts to 30-35 mph.  \n\nHigh temperatures will lower 5-7 degrees Tuesday through Thursday of \nnext week, as the ridge axis slides off to our east, but will still \nbe 7-9 degrees above normal. As the ridge axis shifts to our east, \nthe steering flow turns southerly which may advect some mid-level \nmoisture into our eastern areas (mainly east of Tucson). Measurable \nrainfall chances are very low at this time. However, there may be \nenough moisture for dry thunderstorms and/or gusty outflow winds. \nThunder probabilities for Tuesday through Thursday of next week are \ncurrently in the 5-15 percent range from Tucson eastward. There is \nstill a lot of uncertainty in regards to how much moisture will be \npulled northward and the timing of this moisture.\n\nCPC 8-14 Day Outlooks (May 16 - 22): \nTemperature: Leaning above normal (40%-49%) \nPrecipitation: Leaning above normal (33%-36%) Greenlee and eastern\nGraham counties.",
